I[2]="TEACH answering a call. The Chums call balls 1, 3, 5 face-up; you answer. Aha: answer with the CHEAPEST card that still wins — Hasaan Ali's Wickets 73 is beaten by Starc (79) for a single, so keep Afridy (98) and Pandey for your own calls; a call nothing beats (Inglys' Strike Rate 156, Imaad's Economy 6.27) gets your least useful card. One trap on YOUR calls: Holder's Wickets 66 looks safe but loses to the hidden Imaad or Hasaan Ali (73) — call Guptil's Runs 3531 or Haydenn's Average 51.3 instead, which beat all six. 3 stars: win by 8+ and win 5 of the 6 balls."
I[3]="TEACH the tie pot. Four of theirs (Gurbaz, King, Conway, Najibullaah) have NO BOWLING, and five of yours have none — only Saim Ayuub bowls a little (7 wickets); their two pacers are Ferguson (64 wickets) and Naseem Shaah (33). The rule, plainly: NO BOWLING v NO BOWLING on Wickets is 0 v 0 — a dead heat; nobody scores, 2 runs go into the POT, and whoever wins the NEXT ball takes its runs + the pot. Aha: a tie beats a loss — once both pacers are spent (they grey out in the deck strip), your non-bowler's Wickets call can only tie; then win the next ball (their call) with Buttler, whose Runs 3532 and Catches 86 top all six of theirs."
I[4]="PRACTISE card counting. Their preview shows two hitters among four bowlers: Russell (SR 162) and Saim Ayuub (135). Only Salt (165) out-hits Russell; Maxwel (155) and Markkram (144) beat Ayuub but lose to Russell. Aha: while Russell is in their hand, Strike Rate is sure only with Salt; once Russell is spent (count him in the deck strip), Maxwel's and Markkram's Strike Rate calls are boundaries against the bowlers left. Maxwel's Runs 2754 beats all six — the safe fallback."
I[5]="TEACH the Super Over, alone: the over is already done, level at 10-10, so this duel IS the 3-ball Super Over — your full hand comes back, you call balls 1 and 3, they call ball 2. Aha: with three balls there is nothing to save — lead with your sure balls: Russell's Strike Rate 162, Nabi's Wickets 99 and Rauf's Wickets 113 each beat all six of theirs. Win both your calls and you are also a ball up on the tiebreak (a level Super Over goes to more Super Over balls won)."
I[6]="COMBINE answering + the pot, on the back foot: the Tumblers call first with six batters, and not one of them bowls. Your two bowlers (Naveen 67 wickets, Maharaaj 38) win any Wickets call by a SIX; your four batters never bowl either, so their Wickets call is a sure 0-0 tie — 2 runs into the pot for the next ball's winner, and the next ball is THEIR call. Aha: tie on purpose only when you hold the answer for their next call — de Kock's Runs 2584 and Catches 84 and Amla's Average 33.6 beat all six of theirs — then bank the pot (3 stars)."
I[7]="BREATHER score attack: a Sunday side of tail-enders with one trap, Littoon Das (Runs 2490, Catches 55). Winning is easy; 3 stars need FOUR boundaries in six balls, and the coach shows the path: call Strike Rate with Livingstonee or Phillips and Catches with Pollarrd (a boundary against all but Das), and save Rashhid's 161 Wickets to answer their pacers' Wickets calls for a six."
I[8]="SPIKE v a batting XI (Warner, de Villyers, Raahul, Hetmyre) with two all-rounders (Braavo 78 wickets, Yuvraaj Economy 7.29): their batting calls beat almost your whole hand. Aha: find the three stats where you beat ALL six — Babbar's Runs 4223, Adiil Rashid's Wickets 126 and Imaad's Economy 6.27 — and make those your three calls; answer their batting calls with your cheapest cards (they lose anyway). Trap: Root's Average 35.7 looks safe but loses to Raahul (37.8)."
I[9]="TWIST batters v bowlers: six specialist bowlers call Wickets/Economy on their three balls. Gurbaz, Conway and King never bowl and lose those balls by 2-6, but three of yours bowl a bit: Phillips (Economy 7.35), Ayuub (7.50) and Mahmudullaah (7.36, 40 wickets). Aha: save those three to ANSWER — they beat Economy calls from Shami, Archer or Boult, and Mahmudullaah's 40 wickets beat Shami, Archer and Muraali — and throw a non-bowler at the call nobody wins (Mustafizzur's 136 wickets). Make your own three calls with Gurbaz, Conway and King: their Runs/Average beat all six."
I[10]="BOSS: two overs on aggregate, a fresh hand each over, a Super Over if level after 12 balls — and the Bosses save their stars for over 2. Over 1 is their bench (Holder, Hasaan Ali, Roach, Hetmyre, Rabada, Amirr): de Kock's Runs 2584 and Catches 84, de Villyers' Strike Rate 135 and Hosein's Economy 6.92 beat all six. Over 2 the stars walk out — Raahul, Babbar, Phillips, Imaad, Zampa (127 wickets), Hosein — and only Miller's Catches 82 still beats all six. Aha: the score carries, so over 1 is where you bank: take the widest gap on every ball, then in over 2 lose small with cheap answers and let the lead hold."
I[12]="PRACTISE aggressive with counting. The Foxes' aces by stat: Strike Rate — Suryakumaar 167, Inglys 156; Economy — Vettoori 5.70, Suryakumaar 6.25, Mujeeeb 6.40; Average — Suryakumaar 38.2, Peeter-sen 37.9, Jayavardene 31.8, Inglys 30.2. Aha: Suryakumaar is in all three families, so until he is spent your only sure swipes are Kohli's Runs 4188 / Average 48.7 and the Wickets of Rashhid (161) or Shakiib (149) — no Fox has more than 59. Count him out, then swipe up on the rest."
I[15]="COMBINE v a spin web with an ACE SWING: the cautious Syndicate call first and play their five spinners Safe or Normal — but their ACE, Phill Salt (Strike Rate 165, marked in the preview), is ALWAYS swiped Aggressive. Aha: only Suryakumaar (SR 167) beats Salt, so keep him for that answer — beating an aggressive call pays you +2 and takes a wicket, while any other answer concedes a doubled boundary. The trap: Surya's Economy 6.25 also beats all five spinners, which makes spending him on your own call tempting. Your own calls: Russell's SR 162 beats every spinner."
I[16]="TWIST: the Super Over again (12-12 after the over), now with swipes. Three balls: you call 1 and 3, they call 2. Aha: you hold exactly two sure balls — Inglys' Strike Rate 156 and Maxwel's Runs 2754 each beat all six — so swipe BOTH up (doubled, no risk) and answer their call with whatever wins; Shemsi's Wickets 89 looks big but loses to Afridy (98) and Pandey (94), so never swipe on it. A level Super Over goes to Super Over balls won, then fewer wickets — one lost swipe can decide it. 3 stars: two aggressive wins."
I[17]="BREATHER score attack after the stance drills: three of theirs never bowl (Das, Shanto, Pant) and the other three leak (Economy 8.00-8.36). Aha: every call can be a locked double — Salt's SR 165 and Russell's 162 out-hit all six, and Bumraah's Economy 6.27 beats their best (Southee 8.00) by a boundary: swiped up, that is an 8-run Economy ball, the 3rd star."
I[23]="PRACTISE Free Hit under pressure, against a READABLE rival: two wickets is all out for either side, and the Lords' swipes are scripted — their first two calls of the over go AGGRESSIVE, the third SAFE, whatever the card. Aha: their two loud calls are wicket chances — answer them with your best (Conway's Average 38.1 and Farooqi's Economy 6.90 top all six of theirs) and each win takes a wicket (two = all out); concede their Safe third call cheaply. On your own calls the Free Hit is wicket insurance: put it on your one risky swipe."
I[27]="BREATHER + TEACH Powerplay: one call and the ball after it count double for both sides. A soft side (Hetmyre, Archer, Shami, Shanto) with two real bats, Salt (SR 165) and Kohli (Runs 4188). Aha: trigger it on your surest call and swipe up too — Afridy's 98 wickets beat all six of theirs by a four or a six, so Powerplay x Aggressive makes it a 16+ run ball, the 3rd star. Then mind the doubled next ball: it's their call, so keep an answer for Salt or Kohli in hand."
I[30]="BOSS: two overs, Free Hit + Review + Powerplay once each across BOTH overs, against gamblers who swipe up on any decent card and own a Powerplay. Over 1 is a batting XI (Warner's Runs 3277, Williamson's Average 33.4) with Hasarange's 125 wickets; over 2 is their attack (Rashhid 161 wickets / Economy 6.15, Adiil Rashid 126, Shoayb's SR 147) with Tendulkaar's one-match T20I as the weak link. Aha: plan the powers before ball one — over 2's attack can't bat, so Maxwel's Runs 2754 (their best is 520) is a locked SIX: Powerplay + Aggressive there is 24 runs. Spend the Free Hit on a risky swipe in over 1 (Miller's Runs 2591 loses only to Warner) and hold the Review for their Powerplayed call."
I[37]="BREATHER in Test whites: a soft Test side with ONE Test legend hidden among them (the trap: it out-scores your batters on Runs, so a Runs call into it loses big), and captains on — winning is easy once you count the legend out; 3 stars ask for THREE boundaries (4s/6s) in six balls. Most of your Runs/Average margins land one band short of a four; the captain's +15% (Test Runs and Average also pay x1.5) is exactly what lifts one card's signature stat over the line. Aha: captain for the band, not for the win — pick the batter whose boosted Runs turns a 3 into a 4 on every ball it plays."
I[38]="SPIKE format rotation: balls 1-2 are T20, 3-4 ODI, 5-6 Test, and each card reads its career for the ball's format. Your hand flips with it: Roach is a T20 nobody (8 wickets) but has 284 Test wickets; Stokes has 210 Test wickets and 6861 Test runs; Yuvraaj 8701 ODI runs; Raahul and Mitchell are your T20 bats (SR 139/138). Theirs flips too: Salt (SR 165) is blank in Tests, Tendulkaar played one T20I but owns 18426 ODI and 15921 Test runs, Shoayb has 247 ODI wickets. Aha: sequence the hand — T20 bats on balls 1-2, Yuvraaj on an ODI ball, Roach and Stokes held for the Test balls — and expect Tendulkaar on an ODI or Test ball, never a T20 one."
